Q: How to read a text file line by line in JavaScript? A: <p>Here's a complete solution for reading a text file line-by-line, without using <code>readline</code> or any other libraries...</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>function readLines(file: string): AsyncIterable&lt;string&gt; {
    const rs = createReadStream(file, {encoding: 'utf-8'});
    const i = rs[Symbol.asyncIterator]();
    let cache: string[] = [];
    let lineBreak = false;
    return {
        [Symbol.asyncIterator]: () =&gt; ({
            next: async () =&gt; {
                if (cache.length &lt; 2) {
                    const {value, done} = await i.next();
                    if (!done) {
                        const lines = value.replace(/\r/g, '').split('\n');
                        if (cache.length &amp;&amp; !lineBreak) {
                            cache[0] += lines.shift();
                            cache.push.apply(cache, lines);
                        } else {
                            cache = lines;
                        }
                        lineBreak = value[value.length - 1] === '\n';
                    }
                }
                if (cache.length) {
                    return {value: cache.shift(), done: false};
                }
                return {value: undefined, done: true};
            }
        })
    };
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Usage:</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>for await (const a of readLines('./my-file.txt')) {
    // process the line
}
</code></pre>

Q: Flutter audio_service from a geofence trigger A: <p>The audio playback should not depend on a specific Flutter isolate being alive. The Audio Service Package / Plugin is designed to run in the background as well and it is possible that the isolate was terminated once the app got closed.</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Flutter isolate vs. AudioService</p>
<p>A Flutter isolate is not a reliable long-lived execution environment. The OS is free to kill it at any time when your app is backgrounded.</p>
<p>So audio_service runs the audio playback inside a platform background service:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Android: foreground Service</p>
</li>
<li><p>iOS: background audio task</p>
<p><a href="https://pub.dev/packages/audio_service" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://pub.dev/packages/audio_service</a></p>
<p>Remember to follow the setup procedures inside the plugins page</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Once started, that service is independent of the Flutter UI isolate.</p>
<ol start="2">
<li><p>Headless Flutter engines</p>
<p><code>native_geofence</code> uses a headless Flutter engine to execute Dart callbacks when the app is not running. But again remember that this engine is not the same isolate as your UI isolate. See docs for more details and installation / setup information. As it states, this works when the application is in the foreground, in the background, terminated <a href="https://pub.dev/packages/native_geofence" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://pub.dev/packages/native_geofence</a></p>
</li>
<li><p>AudioService lifecycle</p>
<p><code>audio_service</code> can be started from any isolate, including a headless one. Once started the playback is handled by the platform service</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Here is what you could do</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Initialize and start AudioService from the geofence callback</p>
<p>In your geofence headless callback:</p>
<ul>
<li>Call <code>AudioService.start()</code> if not already running</li>
<li>Obtain the <code>AudioHandler</code></li>
<li>Issue playback commands (<code>playMediaItem</code>, <code>play</code>, etc.)</li>
<li>As being said, do not assume any Flutter UI state exists</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><p>Use a single shared AudioHandler
Your handler must be stateless or reconstructable and any required state (e.g., what audio to play for a geofence) should come from a persistent storage or the geofence event payload</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Regarding a direct answer to your questions:
“Am I incorrect on how this all works?”<br />
your understanding is partially correct. Audio playback via audio_service does not depend on the Flutter UI isolate remaining alive. Flutter isolates are short lived and could be killed by the OS, so audio_service runs playback inside a platform background service, as mentioned above.</p>
<p>“If my understanding is correct, what can I do to make sure the audio service is active when I need it to be?”</p>
<p>You should start / ensure AudioService is running directly from the headless geofence callback. Audio_service can be started from any isolate, including a headless Flutter engine. Once started, playback is managed by the platform service, not the Flutter isolate, so it will work even when the app is backgrounded, locked, or terminated.</p>
<p><strong>Edit</strong>: Here's my AudioHandler's addQueueItems method</p>
<pre><code> @override
  Future&lt;void&gt; addQueueItems(List&lt;MediaItem&gt; mediaItems) async {
    String jwt = await _authTokenProvider.getJwt();

    final newAudioSources = await Future.wait(
      mediaItems.map((item) async {
        File cacheFile = await urlToCacheFile(item.id);
        await updateFileModifiedDate(cacheFile);
        return LockCachingAudioSource(
          Uri.parse(item.id),
          headers: {&quot;Authorization&quot;: 'Bearer $jwt'},
          cacheFile: cacheFile,
        );
      }).toList(),
    );

    final hadQueue = _player.sequence.isNotEmpty;
    try {
      if (hadQueue) {
        await _player.addAudioSources(newAudioSources);
        await _player.load();
      } else {
        await _player.setAudioSources(newAudioSources, preload: true);
      }
    } catch (ex) {
      _logger.e('Error adding audio sources to player', error: ex);
      rethrow;
    }

    final newQueue = List&lt;MediaItem&gt;.from(queue.value)..addAll(mediaItems);
    queue.add(newQueue);

    if (!hadQueue) {
      //If I don't await this call, then it seems like audio cuts out when  
      //the geofence isolate terminates
      await _player.play();
    }
  }
</code></pre>
